92186515468 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515468 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186514931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515468 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9aa2967b-23f5-554f-85ed-580439a3657b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186515365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515480 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b160229e-63c8-5bb3-85c1-a2c73ec30a06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186515157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515508 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ed1fa99e-a491-5eba-bafa-dde6be00551b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186515001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515517 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ffe4c3f0-bc0e-50f0-bcdc-1b3ec7c8a111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515539 #edb/ref 1071 "A total of $200$ colored table tennis balls are distributed among three boxes, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $20$ green balls, while Box $B$ contains $30$ green balls. If $45%$ of all the balls are green, what is the probability that a randomly selected ball is a green ball from Box $C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c96dcfa-0dd7-50ec-b775-86d1b3c50f89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515539 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $G$ be the event that the selected ball is green, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(G\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap A),$ we take the number of green balls in box $A$ $(20)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap A)=\\frac{20}{200}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap B),$ we take the number of green balls in box $B$ $(30)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap B)=\\frac{30}{200}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(G)=45%=\\frac{90}{200}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{90}{200} & =\\frac{20}{200}+\\frac{30}{200}+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{90}{200}-\\frac{20}{200}-\\frac{30}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =20%\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515541 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular park, there are three forests. Forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the park area, and  $30%$ of its plants are poisonous. Forest $B$ occupies $25%$ of the park area, and $20%$ of its plants are poisonous. Forest $C$ occupies the remaining park area, and $6%$ of its plants are poisonous. What is the probability that a randomly selected plant from this park is poisonous? Assume that the plants are evenly distributed throughout the park." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5295db33-6fb5-59ca-9a76-f6dda26f7e03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515541 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $V$ be the event that the plant is poisonous, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the plant is from forest $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the plant is from forest $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the plant is from forest $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected plant is poisonous, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap A)+P(V\\cap B)+P(V\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the park area and forest $B$ occupies $25%$, then forest $C$ occupies the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+25%)=35%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the plants in forest $A,$ $20%$ of the plants in forest $B$, and $6%$ of the plants in forrest $C$ are poisonous, we have\n\n$$\nP(V|A)=0.3,\\,P(V|B)=0.2,\\,P(V|C)=0.06.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected plant in that park is poisonous is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.3)(0.4)+(0.2)(0.25)+(0.06)(0.35) \\\\  & =0.12+0.05+0.021 \\\\  & =0.191.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515554 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515554 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515554 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515554 #edb/ref 1071 "Three bags, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain marbles. Bag $A$ contains $12$ red and $8$ green marbles, while bag $B$ contains $14$ red and $6$ green marbles. A bag is selected at random, and a marble is randomly selected from the bag. If the probability that the selected marble is green is $\\frac{2}{5},$  what proportion of marbles from bag $C$ are green?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515554 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6838f2f1-19a5-5913-bfcd-14e938b0311d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515554 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $G$ be the event of selecting a green marble at random from one of the three bags, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the marble is from bag $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the marble is from bag $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the marble is from bag $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a green marble, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $8$ green marbles out of $20$  in bag $A,$ and  $6$ green marbles out of $20$  in bag $B,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{8}{20},\\,P(G|B)=\\frac{6}{20}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a green marble  from one of the bags is $\\frac{2}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =(\\frac{8}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{6}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(G|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{1}{10}+(\\frac{1}{3})P(G|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{15}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(G|C) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(G|C) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the marbles from bag $C$ are green." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515560 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular national park, there are three forests. Forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the park's total area, and $30%$ of the plants it contains are poisonous, while forest $B$ occupies $25%$ of the park's total area, and $20%$ of the plants it has are poisonous. Forest $C$ occupies the remaining area of the park. If the probability that a randomly selected plant from the park is poisonous is $0.24,$ what percentage of plants from forest $C$ are poisonous? Assume that the plants are evenly distributed throughout the park." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f97d30d-8f92-59ad-aa37-53ebf51b3b4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515560 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $V$ be the event that a randomly selected plant is poisonous, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the plant is from forest $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the plant is from forest $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the plant is from forest $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected plant is poisonous, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap A)+P(V\\cap B)+P(V\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.  \n\n- Since forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the total park land and  forest $B$ occupies $25%$, then forest $C$ occupies the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+25%)=35%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the plants in forest $A$ and $20%$ of the plants in forest $B$ are poisonous, we have\n\n$$\nP(V|A)=0.3,\\,P(V|B)=0.2\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected plant from the park is poisonous is $0.24,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(V)=0.24.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(V|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.24 & =(0.3)(0.4)+(0.2)(0.25)+P(V|C)(0.35) \\\\ 0.24 & =0.12+0.05+0.35P(V|C) \\\\ 0.35P(V|C) & =0.07 \\\\ P(V|C) & =0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $20%$ of plants from forest $C$ are poisonous." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515587 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515587 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515587 #edb/ref 1071 "Mike has a total of $100$ candies divided into three bags. The first bag contains $18$ mint-flavored candies, while the second bag contains $16$ mint-flavored candies. If $55%$ of all of Mike's candies are mint-flavored, what is the probability that a randomly selected candy is a mint-flavored candy from the third bag?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515587 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1ef773a-e0ea-5eef-bbf3-332e3e7efe2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515587 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $M$ be the event that the selected candy is mint-flavored, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected candy is from the first bag,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected candy is from the second bag,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected candy is from the third bag.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap A)+P(M\\cap B)+P(M\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(M\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(M\\cap A),$ we take the number of mint-flavored candies in the first bag $(18)$ and divide by the total number of candies $(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap A)=\\frac{18}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(M\\cap B),$ we take the number of mint-flavored candies in the second bag $(16)$ and divide by the total number of candies$(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap B)=\\frac{16}{100}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(M)=55%=\\frac{55}{100}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(M\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap A)+P(M\\cap B)+P(M\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{55}{100} & =\\frac{18}{100}+\\frac{16}{100}+P(M\\cap C) \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =\\frac{55}{100}-\\frac{18}{100}-\\frac{16}{100} \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =\\frac{21}{100} \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =21%\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515595 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515595 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515595 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515595 #edb/ref 1071 "A certain disease is present in $500$ out of $100\\,000$ people in city $A,$ $800$ out of $200\\,000$ people in city $B,$ and $1\\,100$ out of $500\\,000$ people in city $C.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person from any city has the disease?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3e46311-f49f-5429-9145-049194bfe241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515595 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a person has the disease, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the person is from city $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the person is from city $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the person is from city $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person has the disease, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that there are a total of \n\n$$\n100\\,000+200\\,000+500\\,000=800\\,000\n$$\n\npeople in all three cities.\n\n- To calculate $P(D\\cap A),$ we take the number of people in city $A$ who have the disease $(500)$ and divide by the total number of people in the three cities $(800\\,000).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap A)=\\frac{500}{800\\,000}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(D\\cap B),$ we take the number of people in city $B$ who have the disease $(800)$ and divide by the total number of people in the three cities $(800\\,000).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap B)=\\frac{800}{800\\,000}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(D\\cap C),$ we take the number of people in city $C$ who have the disease $(1\\,100)$ and divide by the total number of people in the three cities $(800\\,000).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap C)=\\frac{1\\,100}{800\\,000}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person from any city has the disease is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =\\frac{500}{800\\,000}+\\frac{800}{800\\,000}+\\frac{1\\,100}{800\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,400}{800\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{8\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1\\,000}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
